        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A concrete paver patio or driveway is the job polymeric sand was invented for: tight 1/8-inch joints, a compacted gravel base and a bedding layer underneath. Almost any bag can work there. The differences that matter are how much haze risk you are buying, how the joint copes with tires, and how much a mistake will cost to undo.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Polymeric Sands for Pavers, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these on what the manufacturers publish (joint range, minimum depth, traffic suitability, dry and cure windows), on color availability for common concrete paver shades, on typical pricing, and on patterns that come up repeatedly in owner feedback. We did not run our own installations or lab trials for this list.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="paver-joints" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Concrete Paver Joints Are Their Own Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            Interlocking concrete pavers are made to tight tolerances, and most have small spacer nubs on their sides that set joints at roughly 1/16 to 3/16 inch. Those joints are narrow and deep, often 2 3/8 inches on patio pavers and 3 1/8 inches on many driveway pavers. That geometry is ideal for fine polymeric sand: a slim column of material, supported on both sides, with plenty of depth for the binder to grip.
          </p>

          <p>
            The narrowness also causes the most common problems. Fine sand has to be worked down into a gap barely wider than the grains themselves, so compaction is essential; without it, sand bridges near the top and leaves voids below. And the paver faces are close together, so any dust left on the surface sits right beside the joint when water arrives, which is where haze comes from.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete pavers also differ from natural stone in how they are built. They sit on a compacted gravel base, usually 4 to 6 inches for patios and 8 to 12 inches for driveways, topped with about an inch of bedding sand. The whole system is designed to flex slightly as one unit, with the joints transferring load between pavers. A polymeric joint helps lock that interlock in place.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your project is flagstone, bluestone or another natural stone with wide gaps, this is the wrong page. Those joints need a coarser wide-joint binder, covered in our general <a href="/best-polymeric-sand/">best polymeric sand</a> guide. Everything below assumes manufactured concrete or clay pavers with tight, consistent joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical consequence: concrete paver jobs are the most predictable polymeric sand installations you can do. The joint width is consistent from one end of the patio to the other, so one product covers everything, coverage estimates are reliable, and the manufacturer's instructions were written with exactly this surface in mind. When a paver job fails, the cause is almost always process rather than product.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="base-check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Check the Base Before You Buy Sand
          </h2>

          <p>
            Polymeric sand stabilizes joints. It does not stabilize a patio. If the base underneath is settling or heaving, the pavers will move and the hardened joints will crack, crumble and let weeds back in within a season. Five minutes of checking before you spend money on sand saves a lot of wasted effort.
          </p>

          <p>
            Walk the whole surface and feel for pavers that rock or click under your weight. Lay a straightedge or long level across the patio in several directions and look for dips deeper than about 1/4 inch over 10 feet. Look at the edges: if the border pavers are drifting outward, the edge restraint has failed. Check for low spots that hold water after rain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Isolated sunken pavers can be lifted, re-bedded with a little more sand and reset before joint sanding. Widespread settling, ruts in a driveway, or edges spreading outward mean the base or edge restraint needs work first. That is a bigger job, but filling joints over a failing base just wastes the sand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Also check slope. A patio should fall about 1/8 to 1/4 inch per foot away from the house so water runs off rather than soaking into the joints. Polymeric sand handles occasional wetting well, but joints that stay damp for long periods soften over time and grow moss on the surface, especially in shade.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you hired the patio out, ask the installer what base depth and edge restraint they used. A contractor who laid 6 inches of compacted gravel with spiked plastic edging has given the joints a stable platform. One who set pavers on a couple of inches of sand over soil has not, and no joint sand will change that. It is worth knowing before you put a weekend into re-sanding.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="patio-vs-driveway" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Patio vs Driveway: Load, Slope and Runoff
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most polymeric sands are suitable for both patios and driveways, but driveways ask more of them. Tires apply concentrated loads, turning wheels scrub the surface, and driveways often collect runoff from roofs and lawns. The joint has to stay locked under each of these forces, which is why we rank more forgiving sands higher for vehicle use.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Factor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Patio</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Driveway</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical paver thickness</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 3/8 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 1/8 inch or more</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Base depth</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-6 inches compacted gravel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8-12 inches compacted gravel</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Joint stress</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Foot traffic, furniture</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tire loads, turning, scrubbing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Runoff exposure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often high near downspouts and lawns</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Our pick</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gator Maxx G2 or HP NextGel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">HP NextGel or Gator Maxx G2</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Budget option</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">PowerLoc or PermaSand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not recommended</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Thicker driveway pavers mean deeper joints, which is good: there is more sand for the binder to lock. But deeper joints need more careful compaction and more water during activation to reach the bottom. Use a plate compactor with a protective pad on any driveway, and plan for the misting to take longer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep vehicles off a newly sanded driveway for at least as long as the bag specifies, and longer in cool or humid weather when curing is slower. Many labels allow foot traffic within a day but ask for two days or more before cars. Park on the street rather than testing the joints early.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where runoff crosses the driveway, such as below a downspout, consider redirecting the water before installing new joints. Concentrated flow is the most common reason joint sand erodes at one spot while the rest of the driveway holds up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Snow removal is the other driveway stress people forget. Steel plow blades and shovel edges catch on joints that sit too high, and rock salt in meltwater can wear the joint surface. Set joints slightly below the chamfer, use a plastic-edged shovel or a plow shoe, and choose a de-icer that is gentle on concrete; our <a href="/best-ice-melt-for-concrete/">ice melt for concrete</a> guide lists options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="depth-chamfer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Joint Depth and the Chamfer Line
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most concrete pavers have a small beveled edge, called a chamfer, along the top. Polymeric sand should stop just below the bottom of that bevel, or about 1/8 inch below the paver surface on pavers without one. Filling right to the top leaves sand exposed on the bevel, where traffic grinds it and water carries binder onto the paver faces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Depth underneath matters too. Manufacturers generally want at least 1 to 1.5 inches of polymeric sand in the joint, and many prefer the full paver depth. On an existing patio, old sand and debris need to come out to that depth before the new material goes in. Shallow fills are a common cause of crumbling joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compaction is how you get sand to the bottom of a narrow joint. On anything larger than a small landing, run a plate compactor fitted with a urethane pad across the surface in several directions after the first sweep. Then sweep in more sand, compact again, and repeat until the joints stay full. Skipping this leaves voids that collapse later.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">QUICK CHECK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Use a putty knife as a depth gauge.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">After compacting, push a putty knife into several joints. It should meet firm resistance almost immediately. If it slides down easily, the joint is not packed. Add sand and compact again before you start cleaning the surface.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Once the joints are full and the level is right, clean the surface completely before any water goes on. That cleaning step, and the watering sequence after it, are covered step by step in our <a href="/how-to-use-polymeric-sand/">how to use polymeric sand</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="color" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Picking a Color for Concrete Pavers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Joint color makes a bigger visual difference than most people expect. A light beige joint on charcoal pavers outlines every paver in a bright grid, which some people like on a formal pattern and others find busy. A grey or slate joint on the same pavers reads as a more uniform surface. Neither is wrong, but decide before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The safest choice is a joint color slightly darker than the lightest tone in the pavers. Most brands offer beige or tan, grey, and a dark slate or black. Polymeric sand also darkens a little when wet and lightens as it cures, so the color in the bag is not the color you will live with.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before committing, fill one or two joints in an out-of-the-way corner, mist them, and let them cure fully. Look at them in daylight, both dry and after rain. This small test also lets you check how your particular pavers respond to the binder, which is useful on older or more porous surfaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy all bags from the same lot if you can, and mix a little from each bag together if you see variation. On a large driveway, a color shift halfway across is obvious. If you are adding to an existing patio, bring a loose paver to the store to compare against the samples.
          </p>

          <p>
            Joint color also affects how forgiving the finished surface looks over time. Dark joints hide the dirt, tire marks and organic staining that collect along the chamfer, while light joints show every bit of it. On a driveway, where the joints will darken with use anyway, a mid-grey or slate color usually ages more gracefully than beige.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Polymeric Sand and Paver Sealer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealing is optional, but it helps joints last. A penetrating or film-forming paver sealer reduces how much water soaks into both the pavers and the joints, which slows erosion and freeze-thaw damage. Some sealers also bind the top layer of joint sand, though they are no substitute for a polymeric joint.
          </p>

          <p>
            Order matters. On new pavers, install the polymeric sand first, let it cure, and then seal. Most sand makers ask you to wait at least a day or two before sealing, and some recommend several weeks so the joint can cure fully and any efflorescence can come out first. Use the longer of the waiting times on the sand and sealer labels.
          </p>

          <p>
            On pavers that are already sealed, polymeric sand can still be installed, but haze is more noticeable and harder to remove from a sealed surface. That is one more reason to choose a low-haze formula like HP NextGel when the pavers have an existing coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Choose a sealer that the manufacturer lists as compatible with polymeric sand. Our <a href="/best-paver-sealer/">best paver sealer</a> roundup covers wet-look and natural-finish options, and our guide to the <a href="/best-water-based-paver-sealer-reviews/">best water-based paver sealers</a> focuses on lower-odor products that suit patios near the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            One caution on wet-look sealers: a solvent-based film can soften or discolor fresh polymeric sand if applied too early, and a thick coat can pool in the joint chamfers. Apply thin coats with a roller or low-pressure sprayer, back-roll any puddles, and keep the first coat light along the joint lines.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bags-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Many Bags and What It Costs
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Planning numbers for standard 4 x 8 inch pavers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">60-100</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sq ft per 50 lb bag, typical range</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3-5</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Bags for a 300 sq ft patio</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$90-$250</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sand for that patio, budget to premium</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">+10%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Extra to buy for spills and settling</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Coverage varies mostly with paver size and joint width. Standard 4 x 8 inch pavers with tight joints usually use one bag every 60 to 100 square feet. Large-format slabs use less, perhaps 100 to 150 square feet per bag. Small cobbles and tumbled pavers with wider joints use more, sometimes one bag every 30 to 50 square feet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Driveway pavers are thicker, so the joints are deeper and use more sand per square foot. Plan on roughly 25 to 40 percent more bags for 3 1/8 inch driveway pavers than for 2 3/8 inch patio pavers of the same layout, then check the manufacturer's coverage chart.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the time of writing, budget sands run roughly $20 to $30 per 50 lb bag and premium sands roughly $35 to $55. On a 300 square foot patio, the difference between the cheapest and the most expensive option is usually $60 to $120. On a driveway that took a crew several days to lay, that difference is trivial compared with the cost of a failed joint.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are still deciding whether polymeric sand is worth paying for at all, our comparison of <a href="/polymeric-sand-vs-regular-sand-for-pavers/">polymeric sand vs regular sand</a> works through cost, maintenance and the situations where plain sand is still the better fit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not forget the tools. A plate compactor rents for roughly $60 to $100 a day at the time of writing, and a urethane pad for it is often included or a small extra. You will also want a stiff push broom, a leaf blower, a hose with a shower nozzle and an N95 mask. Most homeowners already own all of it except the compactor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="old-patio" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Re-Sanding an Existing Paver Patio
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most re-sanding jobs start with an old patio whose plain sand has washed out, grown weeds or filled with ant hills. The temptation is to sweep polymeric sand over the top of what is there. That rarely works. New polymeric sand needs clean joints at least 1 to 1.5 inches deep to bond and hold.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean out the joints first. A pressure washer with a turbo or narrow tip, held at an angle, will blast out old sand and debris quickly, though it makes a mess and can disturb the bedding layer if overdone. A joint-cleaning tool or a stiff wire brush works on smaller patios. Remove any weeds by the roots.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then let the patio dry thoroughly, often two or three days of good weather after pressure washing, longer in shade or humidity. The joints must be dry to the bottom, not just at the surface. This is the step most people shortcut, and it is the leading cause of haze and failed joints on re-sanded patios.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the cleanup as a chance to fix problems. Reset any sunken pavers, tighten loose edges, and clear moss and stains. Our guide to the <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">best surface cleaners for pressure washers</a> covers attachments that clean the paver faces evenly once the joints are out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before refilling, also check the bedding layer. Aggressive pressure washing sometimes blasts bedding sand out from under the edges of pavers, leaving them unsupported. If a paver rocks after cleaning, lift it, add bedding sand, tap it level with a rubber mallet and then proceed. A minute spent on each loose paver now prevents a cracked joint later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When Not to Use Polymeric Sand on Pavers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Permeable pavers are the clearest case. They are designed with wider joints filled with clean, open-graded chip stone so rainwater can drain straight through into a deep stone reservoir. Polymeric sand would clog that system and defeat its purpose, and in some areas permeable installations are required for stormwater compliance. Use the chip stone the manufacturer specifies.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pavers laid on a mortar bed over a concrete slab are another. The joints are often shallow, and the base is rigid rather than flexible, so polymeric sand tends to crack and lift. Those installations are usually grouted with mortar or a product made specifically for rigid systems.
          </p>

          <p>
            Very wide joints, such as those between irregular stones or around curved cuts, may exceed the width rating on most paver sands. Check the bag maximum, and use a wide-joint binder where needed. Our general <a href="/best-polymeric-sand/">polymeric sand guide</a> covers those products.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, if the patio is temporary, or you are happy to sweep in a bag of plain sand every spring, polymeric sand may not be worth the cost and effort. For a fuller list of where it shines and where it disappoints, see <a href="/polymeric-sand-pros-and-cons/">polymeric sand pros and cons</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pool decks are a borderline case worth mentioning. Pavers around pools see constant splashing and sometimes chlorinated or salt water. Polymeric sand is widely used there, but the joints wear faster and need more frequent attention. Check that your chosen product lists pool decks as an approved use, and expect to refresh joints sooner than on a dry patio.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sanding over a base that is already failing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If pavers rock, edges spread or the driveway has ruts, the base is moving. Polymeric joints over a moving base crack quickly and let weeds back in. Reset sunken pavers and fix edge restraints before sanding, or you will be digging out the new joints within a year. Fixing the base costs more up front but it is the only repair that lasts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the plate compactor on narrow joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fine sand bridges near the top of tight concrete paver joints, leaving hollow pockets below. Without compaction those pockets collapse later and the joint sinks. Run a padded plate compactor in several directions, top up, and compact again until the joints stay full. On very small areas, a rubber mallet tapped along each paver will do the same job more slowly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling joints flush with the paver top</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sand left on the chamfer is ground by traffic and washed across the faces by rain, spreading binder and haze. Stop the fill just below the bevel or about 1/8 inch below the surface, then blow the excess off before any water goes on. A little sand left low is far easier to live with than haze across the whole surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Driving on a driveway too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Foot traffic is often fine within a day, but tires apply far higher loads. Many labels ask for two days or more before vehicles, longer in cool or humid weather. Park on the street rather than risking crushed, half-cured joints near the garage. The few days of inconvenience are cheap compared with re-sanding a whole driveway.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using polymeric sand on permeable pavers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Permeable systems depend on open joints filled with clean chip stone so water drains straight through. Polymeric sand clogs those joints and defeats the drainage design, which can also breach local stormwater requirements. Use only the aggregate the paver manufacturer specifies. When unsure, ask the paver supplier which system you have before buying anything.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which polymeric sand suits a paver driveway best?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a concrete paver driveway, Techniseal HP NextGel and Alliance Gator Maxx G2 are the strongest choices. Both are positioned for vehicle traffic and are more forgiving during installation than budget sands. Driveway pavers are thicker, so joints are deeper and need careful compaction and more patient watering. Keep cars off for at least the time the label specifies, and longer in cool weather.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is fine or coarse polymeric sand better for pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For standard concrete pavers with tight 1/16 to 3/16 inch joints, a fine paver sand is the right choice because it can be compacted down into a narrow gap. Coarse wide-joint binders are designed for flagstone and natural stone with gaps of half an inch or more, and they will not pack into narrow paver joints properly. Match the bag's joint range to your measurements.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put polymeric sand over old paver sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not on top of it. Old sand needs to be removed to a depth of at least 1 to 1.5 inches, which most manufacturers specify as the minimum. A thin layer of polymeric sand over loose old sand will crack and lift quickly. Clean the joints out with a pressure washer or joint tool, let the patio dry completely, and then install.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before I can walk or drive on new joints?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the product and weather. Many polymeric sands allow light foot traffic within about 24 hours and vehicles after 48 hours or more, but cool temperatures and high humidity slow curing. Check the specific bag, and when in doubt, wait longer. Keep heavy furniture, grills and planters off the joints during the first few days.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What color polymeric sand looks best with grey pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Grey or slate joint sand usually blends best with grey pavers, giving a more uniform surface. Beige or tan creates a visible grid outline, which suits some patterns. Sand looks darker when wet and lighter as it cures, so test a few joints in a hidden corner and let them dry fully before choosing. Buy from the same lot for color consistency.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I seal pavers before or after polymeric sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On new pavers, install the polymeric sand first, let it cure, then seal. Most manufacturers ask for at least a day or two of cure time, and some recommend waiting several weeks. Sealing first makes any haze harder to remove and can stop the sand bonding to the paver edges. Choose a sealer listed as compatible with polymeric sand.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will polymeric sand stop ants in paver joints?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It helps a great deal. Ants nest in loose sand because it is easy to dig and moves easily to form mounds. A hardened polymeric joint is much harder for them to excavate, so most owners see far fewer ant hills after switching. It will not stop ants entirely if they find gaps at edges or cracks in joints, so maintain the joints over time.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

